          "text": "**Observations**\n\n- *Spatial layout of W1.* The weights are not confined to a central 20×20 box. Large, structured weights appear in the very first image rows (row 0, columns 10–17: e.g. unit 0 ≈ −0.23 at cols 10–11 and 16–17, unit 9 ≈ +0.33/+0.35 at cols 13–14, unit 14 ≈ +0.17/+0.24; row 1, cols 9–18: unit 0 down to −0.37, unit 1 −0.27, unit 9 −0.24) and in the very last rows (row 27, cols 13–14: units 0,1,2 ≈ +0.21/+0.23, unit 14 ≈ −0.31; neighbouring cols 6–10 and 19–24 negative for unit 0). So pixels touching the top and bottom edges, in the central columns, carry real information. The far-left and far-right columns, by contrast, are weak (≈ ±0.03), and the corners are at noise level.\n- *Symmetric \"centre vs. flanks\" templates.* At both the top and the bottom edge, several units have opposite signs at the central 2–4 columns versus the columns 2–4 pixels to either side — i.e. they detect whether a narrow central strip is filled while the flanks are empty (or vice versa). Such a pattern looks like a neckline/collar gap at the top and the gap between two legs at the bottom, not like handwritten strokes.\n- *Mid-image structure.* In the middle rows the weights form broad vertical bands (e.g. units 14 and 15 have runs of ≈ +0.1 to +0.2 over many consecutive rows at roughly the same columns; unit 6 and unit 8 have long negative runs), i.e. large coherent regions rather than thin, oriented stroke detectors. Overall magnitudes are modest and smooth, typical of blob-like, filled silhouettes.\n- *Hidden biases* are fairly large (|b1| up to 0.6), suggesting the units are tuned around a mean image that has substantial mass in the middle.\n- *Output layer.* Unit 15 is negative for classes 5, 7 and 9 only (−1.37, −0.77, −0.75) and positive for all the other seven classes — one unit cleanly splits {5,7,9} from the rest. Unit 5 is strongly positive for 0 and 6 (+0.61, +0.87) and negative for 4 and 7; unit 0 is positive for 6, 7, 8 and negative for 1, 2, 9; unit 14 is positive for 1, 7, 9 and negative for 3, 4, 5. Output bias is highest for class 5 (+0.64) and lowest for class 4 (−0.42).\n\n**Reasoning**\n\n- MNIST, EMNIST and KMNIST are all preprocessed so that the glyph sits inside a centred 20×20 region with ~4-pixel blank margins; the border rows and columns are essentially always zero, so first-layer weights there stay at their tiny initial values. Here the top and bottom rows (central columns) hold some of the largest weights in the whole matrix, so the objects frequently touch the top and bottom edges. That rules out the handwritten-character datasets.\n- Content that spans the full height but not the full width, with left/right columns nearly uninformative, matches Fashion-MNIST's preprocessing (longest side scaled to 28 px, image centred): garments (trousers, dresses, coats, pullovers, shirts) fill rows 0–27 but leave the outer columns dark, while footwear and bags sit in the middle rows.\n- The top-edge \"centre vs. flank\" detectors are exactly what you'd learn to tell a collar/neck opening (T-shirt, shirt) from a filled waistband or high neck; the bottom-edge centre-vs-flank detectors match the gap between trouser legs versus a continuous dress/coat hem.\n- The output layer corroborates the Fashion-MNIST label order: one hidden unit is negative for precisely classes 5, 7, 9 — Sandal, Sneaker, Ankle boot — the three footwear classes, which never touch the top edge and share a horizontal silhouette. Units that pair 0 with 6 (T-shirt/top with Shirt) and that oppose 2/4 (Pullover/Coat) fit the well-known confusion structure of that dataset.\n- Blob-like, smooth weight maps rather than stroke detectors also point to filled silhouettes of clothing rather than thin handwriting.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion-MNIST (Zalando article images, 28×28 grayscale, 10 classes).\n\n**Classes:** 0 T-shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 85%",
